G e n e r a l   A l g e b r a i c   M o d e l i n g   S y s t e m
C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 Fall 2015
      Example 6 in the Lecture notes for 9/29
       
      Minimize cost to supply water by a new treatment plant or wholesale contract
      This is the relaxed version that treats the interger decision variables
      as standard, positive, continuous variables.
      With the additional $35,000 capital cost for establishing the wholesale contract.
       
      Integer version using INTEGER VARIABLES statement and SOLVE as MIP.
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 28, 2015
  16   
  17  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  18  SETS
  19  src irrigation water sources /hd "high dam", ld "low dam", rd "river diversion"/
  20  time season that irrigation occurs in /w "winter", s "spring"/;
  21   
  22  * 2. DEFINE input data
  23  PARAMETERS
  24     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  25           /hd 10000,
  26            ld 6000,
  27            rd 8000/
  28     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  29           /hd 0,
  30            ld 0,
  31            rd 20/
  32    WaterDemand(time) volume of water required for an acre of crop (ac-ft per acre)
  33          /w 1,
  34           s 3/
  35  * river diversion capacity was given in ac-ft/day multiplying by 365/2 gives the semi-annual capacity
  36      Capacity(src) maximum volume of water that can be extraced from a source (ac-ft)
  37          /hd 700,
  38          ld 300,
  39          rd 401.5/
  40      Inflow(time) volume of water flowing into the reservoir each season (ac-ft)
  41          /w 600,
  42           s 200/
  43      Revenue rate of revenue produced from each irrigated acre ($ per acre) /300/
  44  *GroundWater is 2 ac-ft/day multiplied by 365/2 for the gw recharge for 1 time step
  45      GroundWater volume of water that is added to the river flow through groundwater (ac-ft) /365/;
  46   
  47  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  48  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or develop project from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  49            X(src,time) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  50            TPROFIT total profit (revenue from irrigation minus cost to irrigate) ($);
  51   
  52  BINARY VARIABLES I;
  53   
  54  * Non-negativity constraints
  55  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  56   
  57  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  58  EQUATIONS
  59     TotalRev             Total revenue ($) and objective function value
  60     OneDam              Only one dam can be built
  61     MassBalanceRiver         Mass Balance for river diversion
  62     MassBalanceRes       Mass Balance for reservoir diversion
  63     CapacityConstraint  The water from each source must be less than its' maximum capacity;
  64   
  65  TotalRev..                 TPROFIT =E= SUM((src,time), Revenue*WaterDemand(time)*X(src,time))-Sum(src, CapCost(src)*I(src)) - SUM((src,time), OpCost(src)*X(src,time));
  66  OneDam..              I("hd") + I("ld") =L= 1;
  67  MassBalanceRiver(time)..         X("rd", time) =L= Inflow(time) - X("hd",time) - X("ld", time) + GroundWater;
  68  MassBalanceRes(time)..           X("hd",time) + X("ld",time) =L= Inflow(time);
  69  CapacityConstraint(src,time)..    X(src,time) =L= Capacity(src)*I(src);
  70   
  71  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  72  MODEL IrrigationSources /ALL/;
  73   
  74  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  75  SOLVE IrrigationSources USING MIP MAXIMIZING TPROFIT;
  76   
  77   
  78   
  79   


